-    "content": "[\u63d0\u51fa: \u7528\u6236, \u57f7\u884c: Claude] K531: First formal NLP/sentiment pilot. Tested 4 FRED sentiment/uncertainty indices for incremental vol prediction beyond VIX: EPU (Baker-Bloom-Davis daily), Michigan Consumer Sentiment (monthly), TED Spread (daily), HY Spread (BofA). RESULT: MARGINAL \u2014 3/4 pass Harvey |t|>3.0 in-sample (EPU partial_r=-0.048 t=-3.88; UMCSENT partial_r=+0.051 t=4.16; TED partial_r=+0.219 t=18.17) BUT all 4 fail OOS QLIKE (all DM tests NS, p>0.15). Incremental R\u00b2 tiny: EPU 0.11%, UMCSENT 0.13%, TED 2.32%, HY 0.00%. Cross-OOS: EPU wins 3/5, UMCSENT 3/5, TED 2/5, HY 0/5. VIX redundancy: HY Spread 58.9% explained by VIX (most redundant), EPU 13.4%, UMCSENT 12.0%, TED 18.2%. TED Spread shows largest IS effect (partial_r=0.219) but STILL fails OOS \u2014 classic overfitting. VIX sufficiency confirmed #33. Text-based uncertainty (EPU) and survey sentiment (Michigan) add zero practical value for vol prediction. Options market aggregates all public sentiment. Literature: Baker et al. 2016 QJE, Shapiro et al. 2022 JEcon, Ardia et al. 2019. Data: yfinance SPY/VIX + FRED USEPUINDXD/UMCSENT/TEDRATE/BAMLH0A0HYM2, 2000-2026, n=6557.",
