-    "content": "[\u63d0\u51fa: Claude \u9762\u5411G, \u57f7\u884c: Claude] K742: SIMULATION (theoretical). Dollar-denominated crowding analysis of 12/VIX strategy using Kyle's lambda + VIX-SPY elasticity feedback loops. Data: SPY/VIX 2006-2026 (5087 days). KEY FINDINGS: (1) 12/VIX is inherently crowding-resistant \u2014 smooth 1/VIX concave mapping with mean daily |\u0394w|=0.035, annual turnover 8.9x (much lower than binary signals). (2) Feedback loops ALWAYS converge (tested \u03bb\u22640.50, AUM\u2264$500B) \u2014 geometric decay, NOT positive feedback like 1987 portfolio insurance. (3) Worst-day extra impact thresholds (\u03bb=moderate): 5 bps at $0.3B, 25 bps at $1.5B, 50 bps at $2.9B, 100 bps at $5.6B AUM. (4) Sharpe degradation: $0.1B\u2192-6%, $1B\u2192-60%. Friction grows linearly with AUM. (5) Retail adoption (<$1B) has NEGLIGIBLE crowding risk. Even $10B institutional scale, while producing measurable friction, does not destabilize. (6) Publishing 12/VIX is safe for responsible disclosure \u2014 global VT AUM ~$50-200B across ALL variants, 12/VIX specifically would capture tiny fraction. (7) Key protection vs 1987 crash: discrete daily rebalancing (not continuous), concave mapping (not linear), heterogeneous timing in practice. LIMITATIONS: Kyle lambda approximate (0.01-0.50 range), constant VIX elasticity (-4x), assumes simultaneous rebalancing (worst case), no arbitrageur response modeled. Extends K94/K110 (abstract adoption rates) with dollar-denominated AUM analysis. Script: experiments/k742_crowding_simulation.py. Data: yfinance SPY/VIX 2006-2026.",
