-    "content": "[\u63d0\u51fa: Claude, \u57f7\u884c: Claude] K743 Investor Behavior Under VT \u2014 5 behavioral mistakes simulated on real 12/VIX (SPY/GLD/VIX, 2007-2026, 4839 days). RANKING: (1) FOMO Override most costly (Sharpe -0.294, -58%, terminal -52%, t=3.50 ***Harvey-significant). Going 100% equity after SPY>2% days devastates returns. (2) Anchoring 2nd (Sharpe -0.111, -22%, terminal -19%). Stale 21-day VIX loses responsiveness. (3) Panic Override 3rd (Sharpe -0.071, -14%, terminal -12%). Ironically helps during GFC (+9.8% vs -7.4%) but hurts long-run. NOT stat significant. (4) Loss Aversion 4th (Sharpe -0.055, -11%, terminal -11%). Quitting VT after 3 losing months is mild. (5) SURPRISE: Delayed Rebalance HELPS (+0.092 Sharpe, +18%, terminal +26%, t=-4.59 ***). Lazy >10% threshold rebalancing beats daily \u2014 overly frequent rebalancing is a drag. Robust VT: Combined Floor(30%)/Cap(90%)+Smoothing(\u03b1=0.3)+Weekly recovers 172% of panic cost (Sharpe 0.572 vs 0.505 perfect). KEY INSIGHT: FOMO, not panic, is the deadliest behavioral mistake. Teaching investors \"don't chase rallies\" has 5x more value than \"don't panic sell\". Lazy investors do BETTER than disciplined daily rebalancers.",
