-    "title": "K754: Volume Exhaustion Effect \u2014 Extreme Volume Does NOT Predict Lower Future Vol (Resolves K753 Paradox)",
-    "content": "[\u63d0\u51fa: Claude (from K753 finding), \u57f7\u884c: Claude] K753 found extreme volume days (>2x avg) have lowest VIX spike prob (12.6% vs 21%). K754 tests whether this means lower FUTURE volatility. RESULT: OPPOSITE \u2014 extreme volume is followed by 86.5% HIGHER 5d realized vol (t=6.01, p<0.001, Cohen d=0.75). The K753 spike probability was misleading because extreme volume already occurs DURING high-vol periods (mean VIX 25.6 vs 19.3), so VIX has already spiked. The mean-reversion of VIX (5d VIX change -2.40 vs +0.06, t=-4.30) explains the lower spike prob \u2014 VIX drops FROM the already-elevated level, not TO a lower level. Key findings: (1) 95 extreme days in 20 yrs (4.8/yr), 76% are down days, 35% cluster next-day; (2) Forward RV is HIGHER at all horizons (1d/5d/22d all +65-87%); (3) VIX DROPS after extreme vol (mean reversion), but future RV stays elevated; (4) No direction asymmetry (UP vs DOWN extreme \u2192 same fwd RV, t=-0.32); (5) Strategy: vol_exhaust_simple Sharpe 0.59 vs 12/VIX 0.60 \u2014 NO improvement (DM t=0.39); (6) Cross-OOS: wins only 1/5 periods; (7) Effect stable across all sub-periods (2006-2025). CONCLUSION: Volume exhaustion is real for VIX level (mean reversion) but NOT for realized vol. Extreme volume signals ongoing turbulence, not resolution. The K753 finding was a CONFOUND: low spike prob because VIX is already high, not because risk is low.",
+    "title": "K754: Volume Exhaustion Effect — Extreme Volume Does NOT Predict Lower Future Vol (Resolves K753 Paradox)",
+    "content": "[提出: Claude (from K753 finding), 執行: Claude] K753 found extreme volume days (>2x avg) have lowest VIX spike prob (12.6% vs 21%). K754 tests whether this means lower FUTURE volatility. RESULT: OPPOSITE — extreme volume is followed by 86.5% HIGHER 5d realized vol (t=6.01, p<0.001, Cohen d=0.75). The K753 spike probability was misleading because extreme volume already occurs DURING high-vol periods (mean VIX 25.6 vs 19.3), so VIX has already spiked. The mean-reversion of VIX (5d VIX change -2.40 vs +0.06, t=-4.30) explains the lower spike prob — VIX drops FROM the already-elevated level, not TO a lower level. Key findings: (1) 95 extreme days in 20 yrs (4.8/yr), 76% are down days, 35% cluster next-day; (2) Forward RV is HIGHER at all horizons (1d/5d/22d all +65-87%); (3) VIX DROPS after extreme vol (mean reversion), but future RV stays elevated; (4) No direction asymmetry (UP vs DOWN extreme → same fwd RV, t=-0.32); (5) Strategy: vol_exhaust_simple Sharpe 0.59 vs 12/VIX 0.60 — NO improvement (DM t=0.39); (6) Cross-OOS: wins only 1/5 periods; (7) Effect stable across all sub-periods (2006-2025). CONCLUSION: Volume exhaustion is real for VIX level (mean reversion) but NOT for realized vol. Extreme volume signals ongoing turbulence, not resolution. The K753 finding was a CONFOUND: low spike prob because VIX is already high, not because risk is low.",
