-    "content": "[\u63d0\u51fa: Claude (from K743 + research_program), \u57f7\u884c: Claude] K755: FOMO COOLING MECHANISM. Data: yfinance SPY/GLD/VIX 2007-2026 (4839 trading days). Part A: FOMO events (SPY >+2%) occur 8.3x/year (159 events, 3.3% of days). CRITICAL FINDING: next-1d mean return -0.319% (t=-2.07, p<0.05) \u2014 statistically significant mean reversion! Next-2d: -0.35% (t=-1.43, ns). Next-5d: -0.49% (t=-1.35, ns). But by day 20, +1.06% (t=1.86, marginal momentum). VIX regime matters: 67% of FOMO events occur at VIX>25 (crisis recoveries, not genuine rallies). High-VIX FOMO next-5d: -0.83%; Low-VIX FOMO: +0.23%. Part B: 4 cooling variants vs perfect 12/VIX (Sharpe 0.505). Lock-5d BEST: Sharpe 0.527 (+0.022), MDD -0.312 (from -0.323). Lock-48h: 0.524. Gradual-return: 0.526. Cap-increase: 0.505 (\u2248no change). Combined Robust (Lock+Panic floor): 0.524, best MDD -0.306. Bootstrap: Lock-48h vs 12/VIX p=0.057, marginal significance. ALL cooling variants improve vs 12/VIX but NONE beat BH 50/50 (0.547). Sub-periods: cooling helps most during GFC (0.072\u21920.136 Sharpe) and recovery periods. Sensitivity: 1.5% threshold best Sharpe 0.531, MDD -0.298. Lock=10d hurts (0.494). CONCLUSION: FOMO cooling is a real but small effect (+2-4% Sharpe improvement over 12/VIX). Mean reversion after large rallies is genuine (especially high-VIX days). Cooling reduces turnover 5-8% and MDD 1-2pp. As behavioral guardrail it works; as alpha source it's marginal. Consistent with K743 (FOMO costliest mistake) but the systematic cure is mild.",
