-    "content": "[\u63d0\u51fa: Claude (research_program I7), \u57f7\u884c: Claude] K758: Comprehensive analysis of FX hedging for Taiwan investors holding US equities. Data: SPY, 0050.TW, GLD, USDTWD=X, ^TNX 2010-2026 (4003 obs after cleaning yfinance FX corruption). KEY FINDINGS: (1) FX adds only 2.6pp to SPY vol (17.1%\u219219.7%); FX accounts for 24% of SPY(TWD) variance. (2) SPY-FX correlation near zero (0.002) but USD strengthens modestly in crashes (+1.4% annualized on worst 5% SPY days) \u2014 weak natural hedge. (3) Specific crises: 2022 rate hike USD/TWD +14.9% cushioned SPY -24.5% to only -13.3% in TWD; COVID was neutral (+0.7%). (4) Full FX hedging costs 4.9%/yr retail (IR diff 1.1% + NDF spreads 3.6% + margin 0.12%), 1.9%/yr institutional. (5) Hedging HURTS Sharpe: unhedged SPY 0.801 vs hedged 0.736 (cost > benefit). (6) Best portfolio: 40% 0050 + 30% SPY + 30% GLD with 50% hedge \u2192 Sharpe 0.963, but even 0% hedge version scores 0.937. (7) Grid search 330 combos: optimal allocation robust at 30-50% 0050 + 20-40% SPY + 20-40% GLD. (8) Sub-period: 33/33/33 most stable across all 4 periods; hedging helps in low-rate eras, hurts in USD appreciation eras. (9) Practical rec for retail TW investors: 40% 0050 + 30% SPY + 30% GLD, NO FX hedge \u2014 too expensive. Confirms R14's 'do not hedge' conclusion with much more granular cost analysis. LIMITATIONS: yfinance FX data has corruption (cleaned), TNX as US rate proxy imperfect, TW deposit rate assumed flat 1.5%, NDF spreads are estimates.",
