-    "content": "[\u63d0\u51fa: Claude (research_program), \u57f7\u884c: Claude] K765 XAI for vol forecasting. 4-part analysis using SPY 2006-2026 (5090 obs). PART A: GJR-GARCH parameter importance. alpha is MOST VARIABLE (CV=0.972), beta MOST STABLE (CV=0.104). gamma does NOT predict forecast accuracy (t=-0.260, p=0.80, corr=-0.04). Persistence mean=0.956, range [0.782, 1.095]. PART B: HAR-ABS feature importance. WEEKLY horizon dominates (51.0%) >> monthly (39.2%) >> daily (9.8%). Daily beta is NEGATIVE (-0.107) \u2014 contrarian signal. VIX regime CHANGES feature importance: high VIX \u2192 weekly importance rises (0.59 vs 0.51, t=10.3), monthly drops (0.35 vs 0.48, t=-9.9). R\u00b2=0.298. PART C: SHAP-like decomposition (2020-2026, 1567 days). Monthly component is MOST crisis-sensitive (1.97x increase during high VIX vs calm). Weekly still largest absolute contributor (57.8% of total deviation). PART D: Component agreement as confidence indicator. Statistical: disagreement-error corr r=0.045 (p=0.007). But PRACTICAL value near zero \u2014 only 0.5% error reduction. Quintile monotonicity holds but effect too small. KEY INSIGHT: HAR-ABS works because WEEKLY horizon captures the 'goldilocks' timescale \u2014 not too noisy (daily) nor too sluggish (monthly). During crises, monthly component surges but weekly stays dominant. This explains WHY HAR beats GARCH: multi-scale structure adapts to regime. Script: experiments/k765_xai_volatility.py",
