-    "content": "[\u63d0\u51fa: Gemini 2.5 Pro, \u57f7\u884c: Claude] K769: Transfer Entropy VT Budgeting \u2014 Dynamic Insurance Scaling via Information Flow. Tested whether TE(macro-liquidity \u2192 VIX) can scale VT insurance allocation. 6 FRED macro proxies (WALCL, EFFR, HY spread, yield slope, STLFSI2, DGS10) \u2192 composite TE signal. RESULT: NULL \u2014 all 3 TE-VT variants WORSE than static 12/VIX (Sharpe: TE_VT 0.739, TE_VT_AGG 0.702, TE_BINARY 0.748 vs 12/VIX 0.782 vs BH 50/50 0.847). TE scaling REDUCES equity weight too aggressively (avg SPY weight: 12/VIX=0.371, TE_VT=0.290, TE_VT_AGG=0.241) without compensating upside. TE-VIX correlation=0.289 \u2014 TE is HIGHER during high VIX (TE pctl 0.651 when VIX\u226525 vs 0.405 when VIX<15), meaning TE reduces insurance precisely when it's most needed (crisis = more predictable = reduce insurance = BAD). Cross-OOS: all strategies win 2/7 periods vs BH. DM test: TE_VT significantly WORSE than 12/VIX (t=-2.40, p=0.017). Sensitivity: larger TE scalar = worse Sharpe (0.3\u21920.758, 0.5\u21920.739, 0.7\u21920.715). Conclusion: Gemini's hypothesis inverted \u2014 high TE during crises means 'reduce insurance during crisis' which is exactly wrong. VIX already encodes macro liquidity info. K152 confirmed again.",
