-    "content": "[\u63d0\u51fa: \u7528\u6236, \u57f7\u884c: Claude] K772: Hansen & Lunde (2005) overnight/intraday decomposition for SPY (2007-2026, n=4840). Part A: Overnight variance share = 36.8% of total (Hansen & Lunde benchmark ~20% \u2014 difference because they used 2000-era data with less overnight trading). Corr(overnight, intraday) = 0.040 (near-independent). Part B: Overnight share ranges 20-61% across years (2020 COVID peak 52.8%, 2018 low 25.1%). Regime-dependent: high-vol periods have slightly higher overnight share (37.9% vs 32.7% in low-vol). Part C: HAR-OC regression adds r\u00b2_overnight to HAR-RV\u00b2. Overnight coefficient \u03b2\u2084=0.147, t=1.46 \u2014 NOT significant at Harvey |t|>3.0 threshold. R\u00b2 improvement only 0.14% (0.2232\u21920.2248). The overnight squared return adds negligible predictive power beyond what weekly/monthly averages already capture. Part D: Expanding window OOS (n=3828, 2010-2026). QLIKE ranking: AMEM (1.507) > GJR (1.538) > HAR-OC (1.605) > EWMA (1.624) > HAR-RV\u00b2 (1.673). HAR-OC-Ext overfits (QLIKE=162893). DM tests: AMEM beats all at p<0.05 (Harvey PASS vs EWMA/HAR-RV\u00b2/HAR-OC, FAIL vs GJR at p=0.035). GJR beats HAR-OC (DM=-6.62, Harvey PASS). HAR-OC vs HAR-RV\u00b2: DM=1.76, p=0.078 (marginal, Harvey FAIL). KEY INSIGHT: Overnight component is descriptively important (37% of variance) but has near-zero incremental PREDICTIVE power for next-day volatility. GARCH/AMEM already capture overnight info through close-to-close returns. Explicitly separating overnight adds nothing.",
