     "id": "Patton2011-proxy-robust",
     "title": "Patton (2011): QLIKE/MSE only rank correctly if proxy is conditionally unbiased for true vol",
-    "content": "[\u63d0\u51fa: \u6587\u737b\u641c\u5c0b] Patton (2011, J. Econometrics) proved MSE and QLIKE are 'robust' loss functions \u2014 ranking is preserved even with noisy proxy, BUT only if proxy is conditionally unbiased estimator of true volatility. |r| is NOT unbiased for \u03c3\u00b2 (E[|r|] = \u03c3\u00d7sqrt(2/\u03c0) under Normal, different under fat tails). r\u00b2 IS unbiased for \u03c3\u00b2 (E[r\u00b2] = \u03c3\u00b2). This means: (1) comparing models on QLIKE(|r|) favors models that predict |r| natively, (2) comparing on QLIKE(r\u00b2) favors \u03c3\u00b2 models, (3) rank correlation (Spearman) is distribution-free and avoids this problem entirely. K777 addresses this by doing all three.",
+    "content": "[提出: 文獻搜尋] Patton (2011, J. Econometrics) proved MSE and QLIKE are 'robust' loss functions — ranking is preserved even with noisy proxy, BUT only if proxy is conditionally unbiased estimator of true volatility. |r| is NOT unbiased for σ² (E[|r|] = σ×sqrt(2/π) under Normal, different under fat tails). r² IS unbiased for σ² (E[r²] = σ²). This means: (1) comparing models on QLIKE(|r|) favors models that predict |r| natively, (2) comparing on QLIKE(r²) favors σ² models, (3) rank correlation (Spearman) is distribution-free and avoids this problem entirely. K777 addresses this by doing all three.",
     "tags": [
       "Patton2011",
       "QLIKE",
