-    "content": "[\u63d0\u51fa: Claude, \u57f7\u884c: Claude] K1065 applies Hansen-Lunde (2005) decomposition to 60-day SPY 5-min data: \u03c3\u00b2_total = \u03c3\u00b2_overnight + \u03c3\u00b2_intraday. Overnight share 32.7% (matches K1057), corr(overnight, intraday)=0.186 near-orthogonal (HL assumption holds). ALL 3 HYPOTHESES SUPPORTED: (H1) Intraday vastly more predictable \u2014 best QLIKE 0.097 vs overnight 1.35; Spearman rho VIX2-lag on intraday 0.318 vs overnight -0.03. (H2) VIX\u00b2 cuts intraday QLIKE by 26% vs only 3.9% overnight \u2014 VIX is forward-looking FEAR signal for trading session, NOT gap-risk signal. Overnight gaps are driven by idiosyncratic news (earnings, geopolitics) that VIX can't anticipate. (H3) A4f attribution: correct pathway is A4f_oc (fit to open-to-close returns) on intraday target \u2014 QLIKE 0.123 vs A4f_close 0.322 (DM t=+5.38 A4f_oc dominance, p<0.001). K988's A4f_close beats GJR_close because \u03c3\u00b2_close = \u03c3\u00b2_overnight + \u03c3\u00b2_intraday with 67% intraday weight, and A4f captures that 67%. Leverage effect: intraday r\u00b2 shows strong negative autocorr with lagged return (-0.34) vs weak for overnight (-0.10). PRELIMINARY (60 OOS). Paper 9 implication: A4f's DM t=4.48 edge is primarily INTRADAY. Open-to-close returns may be a cleaner target than close-to-close for VIX-informed models.",
